From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000013 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Drive Player <drive-player@localhost>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 21:00:00 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] ps: seek to a time by the timestamps, and show from it when
 precise

DEMUX_GET_TIME reports a time relative to the time track's first timestamp,
and the input seeks with such a time, but DEMUX_SET_TIME took that first
timestamp off it again and moved to that share of the bytes: every seek
landed early by the stream's start offset, and further off by how unevenly
the bytes are spread over the time. Search for the time instead, by the
timestamps of the time track read at the positions the ones known so far
point to.

A picture is decoded from the key picture before it. A precise seek, which
the input asks for unless fast seeking is enabled, lands one to two seconds
before the time and tells the ES output the time to show from: the decoders
decode from there and show nothing before it, as the MP4 and Matroska
demuxers do. A time was sought only once a timestamp had been read, so a
start time took the input's estimate; the length and the time track are
known before the first packet, so a start time is sought the same way.

FFmpeg writes a pack header only every second at 2 Mb/s, and once one is met
the demuxer synced only on pack headers: the probe for the last timestamps
found none in the last 200 kB, the length was left unknown, and the one
estimated from the mux rate took its units of 50 bytes as bytes, fifty times
too long. Probe the end and search on any packet, and count the mux rate in
its units.

Between those pack headers the clock stood still: each packet repeated the
last pack's SCR as the PCR, so the clock moved in steps of seconds, and
pictures were dropped as late around them. After a seek it repeated the SCR
from before the seek until the next pack header. Advance the clock between
pack headers by the bytes read at the mux rate, and after a seek take it from
the time track's first timestamp, less how far timestamps lead the clock.

The time reported was the last timestamp read, which runs ahead of the clock
the pictures are shown by as much as the muxer wrote ahead, most of a second.
Take that lead off, as measured against the clock when a timestamp is read;
until then, as the stream starts.
---
 modules/demux/mpeg/ps.c | 182 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 174 insertions(+), 8 deletions(-)

diff --git a/modules/demux/mpeg/ps.c b/modules/demux/mpeg/ps.c
index 95b4b61..a48b2c7 100644
--- a/modules/demux/mpeg/ps.c
+++ b/modules/demux/mpeg/ps.c
@@ -95,6 +95,9 @@ struct demux_sys_t
     int64_t     i_length;
